WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.330
A writer seals a letter and lets it go.

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:07.930
In 1831, Mary Shelley sent
her novel off with a blessing:

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.940
"I bid my hideous progeny
go forth and prosper."

4
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.860
Three readers open identical copies.

5
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.460
One sees a cage.

6
00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.130
One sees a key.

7
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.900
One sees a heart.

8
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.250
Same words on every page.

9
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.790
So whose meaning is it?

10
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.140
The writer's, or the reader's?

11
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.840
In this mission, you'll map
one text onto three corners:

12
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.290
writer, text and reader.

13
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.930
You'll ask whether a writer's
own comment settles anything.

14
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.910
And you'll read a very
short story by Kate Chopin,

15
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.870
where a single hour changes
everything for Mrs Mallard.

16
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.550
Your mission: decide where
a text's meaning comes from.
